1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:07,392
On the third of February 2021, India signed the largest contract for home-made defence equipment in its history.

2
00:00:07,392 --> 00:00:09,403
Forty-eight thousand crore rupees.

3
00:00:09,403 --> 00:00:12,620
Eighty-three fighter jets, designed and built in India.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:20,216
The Press Information Bureau said the first three would reach the Air Force in the third year, then sixteen a year after that.

5
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:23,997
The third year was 2024.

6
00:00:23,997 --> 00:00:29,195
As of the nineteenth of November 2026, the number handed over is zero.

7
00:00:29,195 --> 00:00:31,391
And here is the strange part.

8
00:00:31,391 --> 00:00:32,855
The aircraft exist.

9
00:00:32,855 --> 00:00:38,493
In September 2025, HAL officials told reporters that ten were already built.

10
00:00:38,493 --> 00:00:41,860
Wings, radar, ejection seats, serial numbers.

11
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:45,382
What most of them do not have is an engine.

12
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,679
So, the useful version of this story.

13
00:00:49,679 --> 00:00:53,546
A country building a fighter is not solving one problem.

14
00:00:53,546 --> 00:00:54,904
It is solving four.

15
00:00:54,904 --> 00:00:58,161
One: shape — airframe, aerodynamics, materials.

16
00:00:58,161 --> 00:00:59,111
Two: control.

17
00:00:59,111 --> 00:01:07,689
A modern fighter is deliberately unstable, because an unstable aircraft turns faster, and that means no human can fly it.

18
00:01:10,667 --> 00:01:19,411
Computers correct it dozens of times a second, and that software, the flight control law, is some of the hardest code anyone writes.

19
00:01:19,411 --> 00:01:23,224
Three: sensing — radar, electronic warfare, seeing first.

20
00:01:23,224 --> 00:01:24,078
Four: power.

21
00:01:24,078 --> 00:01:24,867
The engine.

22
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:31,150
Hold those four, because the next forty years is the story of India solving three of them.

23
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:35,554
Four questions.

24
00:01:35,554 --> 00:01:37,464
Why did India start in 1983?

25
00:01:37,464 --> 00:01:39,506
What nearly killed it halfway?

26
00:01:39,506 --> 00:01:42,470
What is the Mark 1A everyone is waiting for?

27
00:01:42,470 --> 00:01:48,237
And the big one: can a country that builds the aircraft learn to build the engine?

28
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:59,615
By the early 1980s the Air Force's backbone was the MiG-21 — hundreds of them, Soviet-designed, licence-built here, and ageing.

29
00:01:59,615 --> 00:02:05,314
India had two choices: buy the replacement abroad, as it always had, or learn to make one.

30
00:02:05,314 --> 00:02:10,198
In August 1983 the government sanctioned the Light Combat Aircraft programme,

31
00:02:10,198 --> 00:02:15,570
with five hundred and sixty crore rupees for the project definition phase alone.

32
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:27,750
A new body, the Aeronautical Development Agency, would design it; Hindustan Aeronautics Limited would build it.

33
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:29,379
Be clear what that meant.

34
00:02:29,379 --> 00:02:33,201
India had licence-built other people's aircraft for decades.

35
00:02:33,201 --> 00:02:40,406
This was a clean-sheet, unstable, fly-by-wire, composite-heavy fighter, in a country with no modern avionics base,

36
00:02:40,406 --> 00:02:45,856
no composites industry at scale, and no engine house with a working military turbofan.

37
00:02:45,856 --> 00:02:48,412
First flight was targeted for 1994.

38
00:02:55,400 --> 00:03:02,640
Then, in May 1998, India tested nuclear weapons at Pokhran, and the United States imposed a technology embargo.

39
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:07,295
Lockheed Martin and General Electric had both agreed to lend expertise.

40
00:03:07,295 --> 00:03:09,040
Their engineers went home.

41
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:15,223
One Indian account of that period says the withdrawal turned the clock back by four years.

42
00:03:17,367 --> 00:03:19,958
You can read that two ways, and both are true.

43
00:03:19,958 --> 00:03:24,589
It was a serious setback, and a first flight already a decade late slipped further.

44
00:03:24,589 --> 00:03:29,220
It was also the moment the programme could no longer outsource its hardest problem.

45
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:35,970
The flight control work had to be finished in India, by Indian engineers, because nobody else was left to finish it.

46
00:03:40,867 --> 00:03:46,869
India has that capability permanently now, bought at a price nobody would have chosen to pay.

47
00:03:46,869 --> 00:03:52,743
The aircraft flew on the fourth of January 2001 — eighteen years after the programme began.

48
00:03:52,743 --> 00:03:57,257
In 2003 the Prime Minister, Atal Bihari Vajpayee, named it Tejas.

49
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,593
Now the thing most people get wrong.

50
00:04:00,593 --> 00:04:04,149
A first flight is not the finish line; it is about halfway.

51
00:04:04,149 --> 00:04:11,439
Between a prototype in the air and a squadron on alert sit thousands of test flights, weapons trials and two certificates.

52
00:04:11,439 --> 00:04:15,825
Initial Operational Clearance says the aircraft is safe and useful enough

53
00:04:15,825 --> 00:04:20,570
to fly properly — Tejas got one in 2011 and a fuller one in December 2013.

54
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:26,534
Final Operational Clearance says it can do everything it was specified to do:

55
00:04:26,534 --> 00:04:30,190
all the weapons, air-to-air refuelling, the full envelope.

56
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:33,836
That came at Aero India in February 2019, years late.

57
00:04:52,967 --> 00:04:58,203
In between, on the first of July 2016, Number 45 Squadron, the Flying Daggers,

58
00:04:58,203 --> 00:05:03,770
became the first Tejas unit, and Number 18, the Flying Bullets, the second in 2020.

59
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:07,018
Forty aircraft were ordered in that first batch.

60
00:05:07,018 --> 00:05:15,369
As of September 2025, reporting said thirty-two fighters and six of the eight trainers had arrived — an order from the 2000s,

61
00:05:15,369 --> 00:05:18,122
still closing out two decades later.

62
00:05:19,233 --> 00:05:20,648
So what is the Mark 1A?

63
00:05:20,648 --> 00:05:26,368
Not a new aircraft — the same airframe, with the parts that decide a modern air battle replaced.

64
00:05:26,368 --> 00:05:35,508
The Press Information Bureau lists four: an AESA radar, electronically scanned with no moving parts, tracking many targets at once and much harder to jam;

65
00:05:35,508 --> 00:05:43,591
an electronic warfare suite; a beyond-visual-range missile, so it can shoot at what it has not yet seen; and air-to-air refuelling.

66
00:05:51,233 --> 00:05:55,590
The 2021 contract was for seventy-three fighters and ten trainers.

67
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:09,975
Then, on the twenty-fifth of September 2025, the Defence Ministry signed for ninety-seven more — sixty-eight single-seat, twenty-nine twin-seat — at over sixty-two thousand three hundred and seventy crore rupees,

68
00:06:09,975 --> 00:06:13,034
with indigenous content above sixty-four per cent,

69
00:06:13,034 --> 00:06:19,332
and this time India's own Uttam AESA radar and the Swayam Raksha Kavach suite in place of imported ones.

70
00:06:19,332 --> 00:06:21,791
Together: a hundred and eighty aircraft.

71
00:06:21,791 --> 00:06:23,950
On paper, about ten squadrons.

72
00:06:25,067 --> 00:06:29,532
Which brings us to the fourth problem, the one India did not solve.

73
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:33,932
Tejas is powered by the General Electric F404, an American engine.

74
00:06:33,932 --> 00:06:40,565
In January 2021 HAL ordered ninety-nine of them for about seven hundred and sixteen million dollars,

75
00:06:40,565 --> 00:06:44,177
with deliveries due from April 2023 at sixteen a year.

76
00:06:44,177 --> 00:06:47,695
The first arrived in March 2025, two years late.

77
00:06:50,533 --> 00:06:55,740
The Tribune reported that by October 2025, four had been delivered.

78
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:56,200
Four.

79
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:05,006
That is the entire reason an aircraft contracted in 2021 had not been handed over by late 2026 — not the airframe,

80
00:07:05,006 --> 00:07:08,299
not the software, not HAL's factory floor.

81
00:07:08,299 --> 00:07:15,726
In November 2025 HAL signed for a hundred and thirteen more, for delivery between 2027 and 2032.

82
00:07:15,726 --> 00:07:19,472
So: why does India not simply build its own?

83
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:21,176
It tried.

84
00:07:21,176 --> 00:07:26,019
DRDO's Gas Turbine Research Establishment spent decades on an engine called Kaveri.

85
00:07:26,019 --> 00:07:29,939
Reports put the thrust it reached at about seventy-two kilonewtons,

86
00:07:29,939 --> 00:07:37,214
against the eighty-three to eighty-five the aircraft needed, and in 2008 Kaveri was delinked from the fighter programme.

87
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:42,799
That is where the story usually gets told as a national failure.

88
00:07:42,799 --> 00:07:44,979
Understand why it is harder than that.

89
00:07:44,979 --> 00:07:48,947
A jet engine is probably the hardest object a country can manufacture.

90
00:07:48,947 --> 00:07:54,256
Air enters the front, a compressor squeezes it to many times atmospheric pressure, fuel burns,

91
00:07:54,256 --> 00:08:01,430
and the gas leaves through a turbine — and that gas is hotter than the temperature at which the turbine blades would melt.

92
00:08:04,967 --> 00:08:10,321
They survive because each blade is grown as a single crystal of nickel alloy, coated in ceramic,

93
00:08:10,321 --> 00:08:16,528
and cooled by air bled through tiny holes inside it, spinning for thousands of hours without failing once.

94
00:08:21,367 --> 00:08:28,449
You also need the places to prove it: high-altitude test beds, a flying testbed, a certification chain.

95
00:08:28,449 --> 00:08:31,785
India had almost none of that when Kaveri began.

96
00:08:31,785 --> 00:08:32,698
And yet.

97
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:43,788
On the first of October 2026 it was reported that the Kaveri Derivative Engine — the version without an afterburner — had been tested in Russia,

98
00:08:43,788 --> 00:08:47,622
on the ground and then in the air, on a modified Il-76 testbed.

99
00:08:47,622 --> 00:08:52,955
Peak dry thrust: forty-eight point five kilonewtons, against a target of forty-six.

100
00:08:55,100 --> 00:09:00,304
It is meant for the Ghatak unmanned combat aircraft, with certification and integration still ahead.

101
00:09:00,304 --> 00:09:02,005
That is not an engine for Tejas.

102
00:09:02,005 --> 00:09:06,230
But it is an Indian-designed military turbofan that flew and beat its own target,

103
00:09:06,230 --> 00:09:11,012
and that is a different sentence from the one India has been writing for thirty years.

104
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:16,708
So the ambition now runs on three tracks.

105
00:09:16,708 --> 00:09:17,950
One: licence-build.

106
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:26,210
The GE F414, the engine chosen for Tejas Mark 2, is to be made in India by HAL under a reported eighty per cent technology transfer.

107
00:09:26,210 --> 00:09:34,831
That gives India the factory — though defence sources are clear that building someone else's engine is not the same as designing one.

108
00:09:37,733 --> 00:09:38,797
Two: co-develop.

109
00:09:38,797 --> 00:09:47,180
For the fifth-generation AMCA, India wants a new engine of a hundred and twenty kilonewtons or more, designed with a foreign partner.

110
00:09:47,180 --> 00:09:53,686
Rolls-Royce and Safran are the two left in the running, and Rolls-Royce's pitch, reported in June 2026,

111
00:09:53,686 --> 00:10:01,806
offers full technology transfer with the intellectual property staying in India, a core test by 2030 and production by 2036.

112
00:10:03,833 --> 00:10:05,661
No partner has been announced.

113
00:10:05,661 --> 00:10:10,556
Three: build our own, from the bottom up, starting with a drone engine that works.

114
00:10:10,556 --> 00:10:13,328
Watch which of the three the government signs.

115
00:10:13,328 --> 00:10:19,702
That choice decides whether India owns a jet engine in the 2030s, or rents one for another generation.

116
00:10:24,433 --> 00:10:27,733
Why does this matter beyond a factory schedule?

117
00:10:27,733 --> 00:10:28,558
Arithmetic.

118
00:10:28,558 --> 00:10:33,782
The Air Force is authorised to hold forty-two and a half fighter squadrons.

119
00:10:33,782 --> 00:10:42,031
In September 2025, reporting put the real number at thirty-one, falling to twenty-nine as two MiG-21 squadrons retired.

120
00:10:42,031 --> 00:10:48,243
The MiG-21 that Tejas was conceived in 1983 to replace has now left service entirely.

121
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:53,359
Tejas was meant to arrive as those aircraft left.

122
00:10:53,359 --> 00:10:59,936
The old ones went first, the new ones are late, and the gap between them is the squadron shortfall you read about.

123
00:10:59,936 --> 00:11:02,395
Every month of engine delay keeps it open.

124
00:11:02,395 --> 00:11:05,311
That is not an argument against building your own.

125
00:11:05,311 --> 00:11:08,527
It is an argument about the cost of starting late.

126
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:15,309
One more thing belongs here, and it should be said carefully.

127
00:11:15,309 --> 00:11:21,022
On the twenty-first of November 2025, a Tejas crashed during a display at the Dubai Airshow.

128
00:11:21,022 --> 00:11:24,217
The pilot, Wing Commander Namansh Syal, was killed.

129
00:11:24,217 --> 00:11:27,097
The Air Force ordered a court of inquiry.

130
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:40,110
As of the nineteenth of November 2026 it had not reported publicly, and senior officers say the investigation is still under way.

131
00:11:40,110 --> 00:11:45,490
So there is nothing to tell you about the cause, and anyone who tells you otherwise is guessing.

132
00:11:45,490 --> 00:11:49,728
It is here because it happened, and leaving it out would be dishonest.

133
00:11:53,833 --> 00:11:56,349
And here informed people genuinely disagree.

134
00:11:56,349 --> 00:12:06,693
The case for patience: every country that builds fighters took decades and had its own disasters doing it, and India now owns design authority over a modern combat aircraft — airframe,

135
00:12:06,693 --> 00:12:12,285
flight control laws, increasingly the radar — which no amount of buying abroad would have given it,

136
00:12:12,285 --> 00:12:16,107
and which compounds into Mark 2 and the fifth-generation AMCA.

137
00:12:19,233 --> 00:12:24,888
The case for urgency: squadrons are falling now, the threat does not wait for a learning curve,

138
00:12:24,888 --> 00:12:32,903
and a programme that signs a contract in 2021 and delivers nothing by late 2026 has an accountability problem whoever is at fault.

139
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:43,654
Some add that depending on an American engine for an aircraft built to end foreign dependence is the sharpest irony in Indian defence.

140
00:12:43,654 --> 00:12:47,640
Notice what neither side says: nobody argues the aircraft is bad.

141
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:49,692
They are arguing about time.

142
00:12:50,833 --> 00:12:53,765
So, as of the nineteenth of November 2026.

143
00:12:53,765 --> 00:13:00,173
Mark 1: forty ordered, thirty-two fighters and most trainers delivered, two squadrons flying.

144
00:13:00,173 --> 00:13:05,491
Mark 1A: a hundred and eighty on order across two contracts, airframes built,

145
00:13:05,491 --> 00:13:12,813
deliveries not begun — reporting points to the first handovers around January 2027, before Aero India.

146
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:19,376
Engines: four delivered by October 2025 against a plan of sixteen a year,

147
00:13:19,376 --> 00:13:23,700
with a second contract for a hundred and thirteen running to 2032.

148
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:30,712
Production: a third assembly line at Nashik takes HAL's stated capacity to twenty-four aircraft a year.

149
00:13:31,833 --> 00:13:36,117
Mark 2: prototypes in assembly, first flight targeted for 2027.

150
00:13:36,117 --> 00:13:41,471
And the engine: Kaveri's dry derivative has flown and beaten its thrust target,

151
00:13:41,471 --> 00:13:48,062
the F414 is to be licence-built in India, and the AMCA engine partner has not been announced.

152
00:13:50,267 --> 00:13:57,454
If you remember one thing from this episode, make it the three jobs a country has to do to own a fighter aircraft.

153
00:13:57,454 --> 00:13:57,954
Design.

154
00:13:57,954 --> 00:13:58,392
Build.

155
00:13:58,392 --> 00:13:58,829
Power.

156
00:13:58,829 --> 00:14:00,329
India can do the first.

157
00:14:00,329 --> 00:14:01,891
India can do the second.

158
00:14:01,891 --> 00:14:07,641
The third is the whole story — and after forty years it has stopped being an empty outline.

159
00:14:07,641 --> 00:14:09,316
It is half filled in.

160
00:14:13,433 --> 00:14:15,014
Five cards on the wall.

161
00:14:15,014 --> 00:14:22,388
Next on India's Defence, Decoded: INS Vikrant, and what it actually takes to build an aircraft carrier at home.

162
00:14:22,388 --> 00:14:26,441
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

